  {% set alosimPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>eSIM Card</strong> if you know your exact data need and trip length and want a plan sized to match, down to the day. Pick <strong>AloSIM</strong> if you'd rather have fewer choices to make and a free international phone number thrown in. Both sell fixed-GB and unlimited plans, so the real difference isn't the pricing model, it's how granular the sizing is and what's bundled alongside the data.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Dozens of Sized Tiers vs a Simple Lineup</h2>
  <p>
    eSIM Card's United States page alone lists roughly 30 combinations: seven fixed-data tiers from 1GB to 100GB, each sold at several validity windows from 3 to 90 days, plus two unlimited tiers ("Unlimited" and "Unlimited Plus") running 1 to 30 days. AloSIM keeps it simpler: a handful of fixed-GB sizes per country plus an unlimited option, with no separate short-validity discount for buying less time. If you know you only need 1GB for a 3-day layover, eSIM Card has a tier priced for exactly that. If you'd rather not compare that many options, AloSIM's shorter list is easier to scan.
  </p>
  <p>
    That granularity shows up in price. eSIM Card's US 1GB/3-day tier runs $1.92, cheaper than AloSIM's US 1GB at $4.50, but AloSIM's 1GB has a longer minimum validity (7 days) built in rather than sold as a separate short-window tier. They're not quite the same product: eSIM Card is pricing a shorter window, AloSIM a longer one, at that size.
  </p>

