  <h2>Is eSIM Card or AloSIM the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with eSIM Card if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You know your exact data need and trip length and want a tier sized to match instead of rounding up</li>
        <li>You're confident about the plan before buying, since eSIM Card's refunds are limited to narrow cases like a genuine coverage failure</li>
        <li>You want the option to add a virtual phone number or a voice-enabled eSIM from the same app</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with AloSIM if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You'd rather pick from a shorter, simpler list of plans</li>
        <li>You want a phone number bundled in for free instead of bought as a separate add-on</li>
        <li>You want room to change your mind: AloSIM refunds eSIMs that fail to connect or turn out incompatible</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The phone number isn't free on both.</strong> AloSIM bundles a Hushed-partnered international number at no extra cost with a data plan. eSIM Card sells its own virtual number as a separate add-on starting around $2.40, inside the same app as its data eSIMs, so it's a purchase rather than a perk.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Refund policies point in opposite directions.</strong> eSIM Card's own policy describes eSIMs as fundamentally non-refundable once installed or used, with exceptions limited to things like a genuine coverage failure claimed within 30 days. AloSIM advertises a no-questions refund for an eSIM that fails to activate or turns out incompatible with your device, though that window closes once you've made real use of the data on either provider.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>eSIM Card vs AloSIM FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, eSIM Card or AloSIM</h3>
      <p>Depends on the size and length you need. eSIM Card's short-validity tiers (like its 3-day 1GB) are cheaper than AloSIM's equivalent size at AloSIM's longer minimum validity. For a longer single-country stay, compare the specific tier and length on each site, since eSIM Card's pricing shifts by validity window in a way AloSIM's doesn't.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Do both offer unlimited plans</h3>
      <p>Yes. eSIM Card sells two unlimited tiers ("Unlimited" and "Unlimited Plus") at several validity lengths. AloSIM also sells unlimited plans alongside its fixed-GB options.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one includes a phone number</h3>
      <p>AloSIM bundles a free international number through its Hushed partnership. eSIM Card sells its own virtual number as a separate paid add-on starting around $2.40, rather than including one for free.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which has the better refund policy</h3>
      <p>AloSIM's is more generous on paper: a no-questions refund if the eSIM fails to connect or is incompatible with your device. eSIM Card's policy treats eSIMs as largely non-refundable once installed or used, with narrow exceptions like a genuine coverage failure claimed within 30 days.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

